"Effects of toxicants on populations: a qualitative approach I. Equilibrium environmental exposure - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0304380083900194)\nciteturn24search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 43.6 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Equilibrium environmental exposure ... Based on a simple model of toxicant-population interaction, a study was conducted to examine the ecological effects of a toxic pollutant on an exposed population. ... The study supports the hypothesis that the toxicant-population-dose-response system can lead to multiple stable equilibria where, in the absence of the toxicant, the population has only a single stable equilibrium.\n\n## Ecological Modelling\n\nVolume 18, Issues 3–4, April 1983, Pages 291-304\n\n# Effects of toxicants on populations: a qualitative approach I. Equilibrium environmental exposure\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/0304-3800(83)90019-4Get rights and content\n\nComplimentary access\n\n## Abstract\n\nTheoretical models that examine the environmental fate of a toxicant are available but few mathematical models have been developed that are concerned with the effects of a toxicant on an ecosystem. Based on a simple model of toxicant-population interaction, a study was conducted to examine the ecological effects of a toxic pollutant on an exposed population. The model, consisting of a system of ordinary differential equations, employs an equation to represent the dynamics of the population, an equation to represent the dynamics of the concentration of the toxicant and a coupling of the toxicant-population interaction through generic dose-response functions. The role in the model of population density dependence, its allocation to birth and death processes and the response of these parameters to toxic stress is investigated. Conclusions, obtained from the model by analytical techniques, are directed toward modes of persistence and extinction. The study supports the hypothesis that the toxicant-population-dose-response system can lead to multiple stable equilibria where, in the absence of the toxicant, the population has only a single stable equilibrium.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSocial Networks and Intraspeaker Variation During Periods (https://repository.upenn.edu/bitstreams/d7a60da5-c8ff-45ff-b874-4ca9ef9893db/download)\nciteturn24search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Social Networks and Intraspeaker Variation During Periods ... Celina Troutman, Brady Clark, and Matthew Goldrick ... social network of language users.\nSocial Networks and Intraspeaker Variation During Periods \nof Language Change \n \nCelina Troutman, Brady Clark, and Matthew Goldrick \n \n1  Introduction \n \nPrevious work has revealed general characteristics of language change at \nboth the level of linguistic communities as well as individual speakers. What \nare the properties of language users such that we can account for these char-\nacteristics? To address this question, we built a computational model of a \nsocial network of language users. By holding the network structure constant \nand varying properties of the language users, we found that language change \nreflects both the structure of social networks and properties of language us-\ners. In particular, our results suggest that although language users must be \ncapable of probabilistically accessing multiple grammars, they must prefer to \naccess a single grammar categorically. \n \n1.1  Characteristics of Language Change \n \nTo ground our discussion of language change, consider the rise of periphras-\ntic do (or do support) in English (Ellegård 1953, Kroch 1989, Warner 2004). \nPrior to about 1400, negative declarative sentences were formed by follow-\ning a simple finite verb with not, as in (1). This was followed by a period of \nvariation from 1400 to 1800 between the older form and the modern form \nwith periphrastic do. Importantly, during this time both the older and modern \nforms were available for a single person, as illustrated in (2).  \n \n (1) …whiche he perceiueth not. \n  \n(cited in Kroch 1989:15) \n (2) a.  I question not your friendship… \n  \n \n(Thomas Otway, “The Cheats of Scapin”, 1676/7) \n  \nb.   She does not deserve it… \n  \n \n(Thomas Otway, “Friendship in Fashion”, 1678) \n  \n \n(cited in Warner 2004: 229) \n \nThis paper focuses on the following general characteristics of language \nchange, each of which is illustrated by the development of periphrastic do: \nU. Penn Working Papers in Linguistics, Volume 14.1, 2008--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInductive Reasoning, Bounded Rationality and the Bar Problem (https://sfi-edu.s3.amazonaws.com/sfi-edu/production/uploads/sfi-com/dev/uploads/filer/eb/8d/eb8d7d4d-8bc0-49ac-8feb-e73c5a141a12/94-03-014.pdf)\nciteturn24search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Inductive Reasoning, Bounded Rationality and the Bar Problem ... www.santafe.edu\nInductive Reasoning, Bounded Rationality and the Bar Problem\n\nW. Brian Arthur\n\nSFI WORKING PAPER: 1994-03-014\n\nSFI Working Papers contain accounts of scientific work of the author(s) and do not necessarily represent the views of the Santa Fe Institute. We accept papers intended for publication in peer-reviewed journals or proceedings volumes, but not papers that have already appeared in print. Except for papers by our external faculty, papers must be based on work done at SFI, inspired by an invited visit to or collaboration at SFI, or funded by an SFI grant.\n\n©NOTICE: This working paper is included by permission of the contributing author(s) as a means to ensure timely distribution of the scholarly and technical work on a non-commercial basis. Copyright and all rights therein are maintained by the author(s). It is understood that all persons copying this information will adhere to the terms and constraints invoked by each author's copyright. These works may be reposted only with the explicit permission of the copyright holder.\n\nwww.santafe.edu--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEconPapers: Inductive Reasoning, Bounded Rationality and the Bar Problem (https://econpapers.repec.org/paper/wopsafiwp/94-03-014.htm)\nciteturn24search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 32.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Inductive Reasoning, Bounded Rationality and the Bar Problem ... Brian Arthur Working Papers from Santa Fe Institute Abstract: This paper draws on modern psychology to argue that as humans, in economic decision contexts that are complicated or ill-defined, we use not deductive, but inductive reasoning.\n\n# Inductive Reasoning, Bounded Rationality and the Bar Problem\n\nW. Brian Arthur Working Papers from Santa Fe Institute Abstract: This paper draws on modern psychology to argue that as humans, in economic decision contexts that are complicated or ill-defined, we use not deductive, but inductive reasoning. That is, in such contexts we induce a variety of working hypotheses or mental models, act upon the most credible, and replace hypotheses with new ones if they cease to work. Inductive reasoning leads to a rich psychological world in which an agent's hypotheses or mental models compete for survival against each other, in an environment formed by other agents' hypotheses or mental models---a world that is both evolutionary and complex. Inductive reasoning can be modeled in a variety of ways. The main body of the paper introduces and models a coordination problem---``the bar problem''---in which agents' expectations are forced to be subjective and to differ. It shows that while agents' beliefs never settle down, collectively they form and ``ecology\" that does converge to an equilibrium pattern. Date: 1994-03--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRead \"Cities and Their Vital Systems: Infrastructure Past, Present, and Future\" at NA